    "reading": "compares B and C against A on cells where the goal-only call did NOT run, at the same seed. Identity there is the structural proof that the knobs contribute exactly zero outside the split-induce minority.",
    "B_vs_C_control_comparison": {
      "n_compared": 2,
      "n_identical": 2,
      "disagreeing": []
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": "THE CHECK FIRED, AND IT FOUND A HARNESS DEFECT RATHER THAN CONFIRMING THE ASSUMPTION. A-vs-treatment identity holds on ar25 and FAILS on bp35 (A 3722 bytes / sha bcf42b01, B and C both 4150 bytes / sha 64b7c69b) -- same seed 8300, zero content failures, and the goal-only call ran in NONE of the three, so neither knob could have acted. B and C are byte-identical to EACH OTHER on both games, and they differ in the dedup knob, so the divergence is not knob-driven. What differs between A and (B, C) is POSITION: A is the first cell of the game block and B and C follow it against a KV cache warmed by the identical preceding request. CONSEQUENCE: a fixed CARNOT_ARC_GENERATOR_SEED does NOT guarantee a byte-identical completion on this server -- llama.cpp's sampled output depends on cache/batch state as well as the seed. The pairing this design relies on is therefore APPROXIMATE, not exact, and non-firing cells contribute NUISANCE VARIATION rather than the exact zero the pre-run reasoning assumed. Reported rather than dropped: this is precisely the outcome the check was written to surface, and it makes the arm-order confound a SHAPE issue and not only a timing one."
  },
